00:11:37,461 --> 00:11:44,932
Right, Niamh, I finally got all the
responses into one spreadsheet, 412 in total.

203
00:11:44,969 --> 00:11:46,560
Not bad at all.

204
00:11:46,561 --> 00:11:48,500
Fewer than we hoped, mind you.

205
00:11:48,501 --> 00:11:50,840
I think the questionnaire
was just too long.

206
00:11:51,289 --> 00:11:54,060
12 pages was asking a lot of people.

207
00:11:54,309 --> 00:11:56,840
So, where did most of
them come from in the end?

208
00:11:56,841 --> 00:11:57,840
Online?

209
00:11:57,841 --> 00:12:02,100
That's what I assumed too, given how
much we pushed the link on social media.

210
00:12:02,399 --> 00:12:05,980
But no, the online
version only got about 130.

211
00:12:06,179 --> 00:12:10,620
The paper copies did far better, and
nearly all of those came from libraries.

212
00:12:10,621 --> 00:12:11,621
Really?

213
00:12:11,719 --> 00:12:13,300
Not the doctor's surgeries?

214
00:12:13,449 --> 00:12:16,740
We got a grand total
of 19 from the surgeries.

215
00:12:16,889 --> 00:12:20,460
And the supermarkets wouldn't let us leave
any in the first place, if you remember.

216
00:12:20,709 --> 00:12:23,580
Oh yes, I remember the manager's face.

217
00:12:24,429 --> 00:12:28,700
And to think we spent a whole
weekend designing that online version.

218
00:12:28,701 --> 00:12:32,160
Still, at least the paper
ones were properly filled in.

219
00:12:32,161 --> 00:12:34,740
Half the online people
gave up after page three.

220
00:12:34,939 --> 00:12:37,860
Yeah, the completion rate
on paper was much better.

221
00:12:37,861 --> 00:12:38,861
Anyway.

222
00:12:38,910 --> 00:12:39,910
Okay.

223
00:12:40,109 --> 00:12:41,860
So, the big question.

224
00:12:42,209 --> 00:12:44,900
Why do the drivers say
they don't use the buses?

225
00:12:45,349 --> 00:12:47,320
I'd have bet money it'd be the fares.

226
00:12:47,719 --> 00:12:48,900
So would I.

227
00:12:48,901 --> 00:12:50,860
But cost only came third.

228
00:12:51,059 --> 00:12:52,860
It isn't the price that puts them off.

229
00:12:52,959 --> 00:12:55,820
And it isn't really the journey
time, which was second.

230
00:12:56,169 --> 00:13:00,940
By some distance, the thing drivers
kept coming back to was reliability.

231
00:13:00,990 --> 00:13:04,060
Buses not turning up
or two arriving at once.

232
00:13:04,209 --> 00:13:06,180
That fits with the
comments section, actually.

233
00:13:06,181 --> 00:13:09,180
People were furious about
the number seven route.

234
00:13:09,929 --> 00:13:11,180
What about the cyclists?

235
00:13:11,579 --> 00:13:14,220
I was expecting pages
about dangerous traffic.

236
00:13:14,619 --> 00:13:18,300
There was some of that, and a
fair few mentioned showers at work.

237
00:13:18,849 --> 00:13:21,900
But the real complaint wasn't
safety on the roads at all.

238
00:13:22,099 --> 00:13:24,220
It was that there's
nowhere to leave a bike.

239
00:13:24,369 --> 00:13:27,173
They want secure storage,
at the station and at

240
00:13:27,197 --> 00:13:30,312
work, because so many of
them have had bikes stolen.

241
00:13:30,313 --> 00:13:31,340
Right.

242
00:13:31,439 --> 00:13:32,860
And the over 60s?

243
00:13:32,959 --> 00:13:37,180
I suppose price doesn't matter much
to them, since most of them travel free.

244
00:13:37,181 --> 00:13:38,220
Exactly.

245
00:13:38,319 --> 00:13:39,980
Cost hardly featured.

246
00:13:40,079 --> 00:13:43,340
And interestingly, speed
wasn't a big deal either.

247
00:13:43,439 --> 00:13:45,763
When we asked them
to rank things, they put

248
00:13:45,787 --> 00:13:48,232
comfort above journey
time almost every time.

249
00:13:48,719 --> 00:13:51,980
Getting a seat, a smooth
ride, that sort of thing.

250
00:13:52,129 --> 00:13:53,980
Which surprised me a bit, actually.

251
00:13:54,079 --> 00:13:56,298
I'd assumed reliability
would top their list

252
00:13:56,322 --> 00:13:58,552
as well, the same as
it did for the drivers.

253
00:13:58,689 --> 00:14:00,460
But it only came third for them.

254
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:02,060
That's a nice finding.

255
00:14:02,159 --> 00:14:03,900
Now, presentation.

256
00:14:03,999 --> 00:14:06,300
Dr. Ellis said tables, didn't she?

257
00:14:06,399 --> 00:14:09,900
She did say tables were
fine for the appendix.

258
00:14:10,049 --> 00:14:12,460
And I'd started doing
bar charts for everything.

259
00:14:12,560 --> 00:14:15,660
But so much of this is
about where people live.

260
00:14:15,709 --> 00:14:19,100
I really think the main
findings should go on maps.

261
00:14:19,150 --> 00:14:22,220
You'd see the gaps in the
bus network straight away.

262
00:14:22,221 --> 00:14:23,660
Yeah, you're right.

263
00:14:24,270 --> 00:14:25,900
Maps for the main results, then.

264
00:14:25,999 --> 00:14:27,660
I'll ditch the bar charts.

265
00:14:27,759 --> 00:14:30,060
Before you hear the rest of the discussion,

266
00:14:30,209 --> 00:14:34,380
you have some time to
look at questions 26 to 30.

267
00:14:42,670 --> 00:14:50,620
Now listen and answer questions 26 to 30.

268
00:14:59,329 --> 00:15:01,580
We need to be honest
about the sample, though.

269
00:15:01,779 --> 00:15:03,900
Dr. Ellis will go straight for it.

270
00:15:03,999 --> 00:15:05,100
I know.

271
00:15:05,249 --> 00:15:07,100
My worry's the young people.

272
00:15:07,249 --> 00:15:09,900
I thought we'd hardly have any under 25.

273
00:15:09,999 --> 00:15:11,340
We're actually fine there.

274
00:15:11,439 --> 00:15:15,260
They're about 22%, which is
close to the city as a whole.

275
00:15:15,359 --> 00:15:18,620
And before you say it,
students aren't a problem either.

276
00:15:18,819 --> 00:15:20,220
There were only about 40 of them.

277
00:15:21,069 --> 00:15:22,140
No, you're right.

278
00:15:22,929 --> 00:15:25,740
The thing that does
bother me is the postcodes.

279
00:15:25,839 --> 00:15:29,740
Nearly two-thirds of the responses
are from the eastern side of the city,

280
00:15:29,839 --> 00:15:32,140
because that's where
the libraries we used are.

281
00:15:32,239 --> 00:15:34,220
That's the real weakness, isn't it?

282
00:15:34,369 --> 00:15:36,620
We can't say much about the west at all.

283
00:15:36,621 --> 00:15:37,621
Agreed.

284
00:15:37,789 --> 00:15:39,660
What about the working from home question?

285
00:15:40,270 --> 00:15:43,820
A lot of people have said that's
the most surprising part of the data.

286
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:46,300
I don't think it surprised
anyone, to be honest.

287
00:15:46,399 --> 00:15:48,540
It's what every report's
been saying for years.

288
00:15:49,279 --> 00:15:51,020
My problem with it is the wording.

289
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:54,140
We asked how often people work remotely,

290
00:15:54,289 --> 00:15:58,460
and half of them didn't know whether to
count a morning at home as a day or not.

291
00:15:58,560 --> 00:16:01,900
You can see they've interpreted
it in completely different ways.

292
00:16:01,949 --> 00:16:05,660
Would it have helped if it had
come earlier, before people got tired?

293
00:16:05,759 --> 00:16:08,220
I don't think where it
was made any difference.

294
00:16:08,319 --> 00:16:09,980
It's the way we phrased it.

295
00:16:09,981 --> 00:16:10,981
Fair enough.

296
00:16:11,180 --> 00:16:13,420
Now, the follow-up interviews.

297
00:16:13,569 --> 00:16:16,060
You know I was against
putting them in at first.

298
00:16:16,109 --> 00:16:18,700
You said eight interviews
weren't worth the paper.

299
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:20,500
I did.

300
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:25,340
And it wasn't Dr. Ellis who changed my
mind either, although she did suggest them.

301
00:16:25,439 --> 00:16:28,060
And they certainly don't
fix the response rate.

302
00:16:28,159 --> 00:16:29,820
Eight people isn't going to do that.

303
00:16:30,529 --> 00:16:34,780
It's that they make sense of those
odd figures for the occasional bus users.

304
00:16:34,879 --> 00:16:38,300
The ones who say they hate
the buses and use them anyway.

305
00:16:38,399 --> 00:16:40,220
The interviews show why.

306
00:16:40,221 --> 00:16:40,886
Great!

307
00:16:40,910 --> 00:16:41,980
So they're in.

308
00:16:42,079 --> 00:16:43,740
And the national census figures?

309
00:16:43,839 --> 00:16:46,620
I'd love to use them to
back up our main argument.

310
00:16:46,719 --> 00:16:49,740
The trouble is, their
categories don't match ours.

311
00:16:50,369 --> 00:16:53,500
They lump cycling and
walking together, for one thing.

312
00:16:53,599 --> 00:16:57,500
And they're from four years ago,
before all the changes to the bus routes.

313
00:16:57,649 --> 00:17:00,140
So any comparison
would be a bit misleading.

314
00:17:01,550 --> 00:17:03,820
I don't want to bin
them completely, though.

315
00:17:03,970 --> 00:17:05,579
They took ages to find.

316
00:17:05,729 --> 00:17:08,562
How about we stick them
at the back, as supporting

317
00:17:08,586 --> 00:17:11,020
material, but don't
build anything on them?

318
00:17:11,021 --> 00:17:11,740
Okay.

319
00:17:11,741 --> 00:17:12,780
An appendix it is.

320
00:17:13,389 --> 00:17:16,213
So, before we see Dr. Ellis
on Friday, should we

321
00:17:16,237 --> 00:17:19,192
email the bus company
for their passenger numbers?

322
00:17:19,679 --> 00:17:21,740
They'd take weeks to reply.

323
00:17:21,889 --> 00:17:24,380
And there's no time to set
up more interviews, either.

324
00:17:24,479 --> 00:17:26,859
Then let's go back through
the written comments.

325
00:17:26,959 --> 00:17:29,900
I noticed we've coded some of
them differently from each other,

326
00:17:30,049 --> 00:17:31,980
so we should sort that out first.

327
00:17:31,981 --> 00:17:33,020
Good idea.

328
00:17:33,119 --> 00:17:35,362
I'll take the first half,
you take the second,

329
00:17:35,386 --> 00:17:37,592
and we can compare
notes on Thursday evening.

330
00:17:38,509 --> 00:17:41,340
Then at least we'll walk in on
Friday agreeing with each other.

331
00:17:42,399 --> 00:17:44,060
That is the end of part three.

332
00:17:44,749 --> 00:17:48,620
You now have one minute to
check your answers to part three.

333
00:18:16,739 --> 00:18:17,740
Part four.

334
00:18:18,639 --> 00:18:23,112
You will hear a lecturer giving a talk
about the engineering of suspension bridges.

335
00:18:23,839 --> 00:18:27,820
First, you have some time
to look at questions 31 to 40.

336
00:18:28,379 --> 00:18:32,380
Now, listen carefully and
answer questions 31 to 40.

337
00:18:38,319 --> 00:18:39,980
Okay, let's get started.

338
00:18:40,079 --> 00:18:42,338
Today, we're looking
at suspension bridges,

339
00:18:42,362 --> 00:18:46,300
which, for the longest spans, are still
pretty much the only option we have.

340
00:18:47,009 --> 00:18:51,260
I want to focus on how they actually
carry loads, and then on what can go wrong.

341
00:18:51,869 --> 00:18:54,218
Just to put that in
context, a good concrete

342
00:18:54,242 --> 00:18:56,867
beam bridge might span
a couple of hundred meters

343
00:18:56,868 --> 00:19:00,380
between supports, and a
cable-stayed bridge perhaps a thousand.

344
00:19:01,009 --> 00:19:05,500
The longest suspension bridges now cross
more than two kilometers in a single span.

345
00:19:06,129 --> 00:19:09,082
So whatever they're doing,
they're doing it very efficiently,

346
00:19:09,083 --> 00:19:12,220
and the reason comes down to
how each part shares the work.

347
00:19:12,799 --> 00:19:15,647
The basic idea is simple:
the deck, the part you

348
00:19:15,671 --> 00:19:18,467
drive on, hangs from
vertical cables, and those

349
00:19:18,468 --> 00:19:22,780
vertical cables hang from two main cables
that run over the tops of the towers.

350
00:19:23,439 --> 00:19:27,592
Now, a lot of students assume the main
cables are being squeezed by all that weight.

351
00:19:28,140 --> 00:19:28,985
They aren't.

352
00:19:29,009 --> 00:19:31,580
A cable can't resist being squeezed at all.

353
00:19:31,729 --> 00:19:34,140
The main cables are only ever in tension.

354
00:19:34,289 --> 00:19:35,500
They're being pulled.

355
00:19:35,599 --> 00:19:38,275
It's the towers that work
in compression, pushing

356
00:19:38,299 --> 00:19:40,712
the load straight down
into the foundations.

357
00:19:40,849 --> 00:19:42,940
So where does the pull
in the cables end up?

358
00:19:43,539 --> 00:19:47,180
At each end of the bridge, the
cables are tied into anchorages.

359
00:19:47,279 --> 00:19:49,883
These are enormous blocks,
and their whole job is to

360
00:19:49,907 --> 00:19:52,472
be heavy enough not to
be dragged toward the river.

361
00:19:53,059 --> 00:19:57,340
On older bridges, they were built of
masonry, cut stone, and in a few places,

362
00:19:57,341 --> 00:20:00,300
the cables were simply
anchored into solid rock.

363
00:20:00,449 --> 00:20:04,300
But today, almost without exception,
anchorages are made of concrete,

364
00:20:04,449 --> 00:20:07,180
sometimes hundreds
of thousands of tons of it.

365
00:20:07,279 --> 00:20:09,980
The main cables themselves are fascinating.

366
00:20:10,079 --> 00:20:13,500
You might picture a giant
twisted rope, like a ship's hawser.

367
00:20:13,599 --> 00:20:15,180
That's not how it's done.

368
00:20:15,279 --> 00:20:18,179
Each cable is made of
thousands of thin, parallel

369
00:20:18,203 --> 00:20:21,352
steel wires, and they're
laid in place one at a time,

370
00:20:21,439 --> 00:20:23,475
high above the water,
by a wheel running

371
00:20:23,499 --> 00:20:25,752
backward and forward
between the anchorages.

372
00:20:26,189 --> 00:20:29,192
The process looks a bit
like weaving, but engineers

373
00:20:29,216 --> 00:20:32,232
call it spinning, and it
can take more than a year.

374
00:20:32,319 --> 00:20:34,380
Let's think about the shape of the cable.

375
00:20:34,479 --> 00:20:37,502
If you hang a chain between
two points, with nothing

376
00:20:37,526 --> 00:20:40,152
attached, it forms a
curve called a catenary.

377
00:20:40,239 --> 00:20:42,940
You'll see that word in older
textbooks applied to bridges.

378
00:20:43,089 --> 00:20:46,780
But a suspension bridge cable
isn't just hanging under its own.

379
00:20:54,829 --> 00:20:59,580
Once the deck is attached, and the
load is spread evenly along the span,

380
00:20:59,679 --> 00:21:02,363
the curve is much
closer to a parabola, and

381
00:21:02,387 --> 00:21:05,272
that's the shape designers
actually work with.

382
00:21:05,309 --> 00:21:07,180
Here's a detail I love.

383
00:21:07,279 --> 00:21:11,580
On the very longest bridges,
the towers aren't exactly parallel.

384
00:21:11,729 --> 00:21:15,500
They're a few centimeters farther
apart at the top than at the bottom.

385
00:21:15,599 --> 00:21:20,060
It's not a construction error, and it's not
the cables pulling them outward either.

386
00:21:20,159 --> 00:21:23,660
It's because each tower points
toward the center of the earth,

387
00:21:23,809 --> 00:21:28,460
so the curvature of the planet means they
lean very slightly away from each other.

388
00:21:28,460 --> 00:21:30,300
Now, what can go wrong?

389
00:21:30,399 --> 00:21:32,300
The big enemy is wind.

390
00:21:32,449 --> 00:21:35,225
You've probably all seen
the film of the Tacoma

391
00:21:35,249 --> 00:21:38,392
Narrows Bridge in 1940,
twisting until it fell apart.

392
00:21:38,529 --> 00:21:40,867
You'll often read that
it was destroyed by

393
00:21:40,891 --> 00:21:43,352
resonance, like a
singer shattering a glass.

394
00:21:43,389 --> 00:21:45,100
That's not really accurate.

395
00:21:46,659 --> 00:21:50,476
Engineers now agree the cause was flutter,
a self-feeding twisting motion, where

396
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:54,472
the movement of the deck changes the airflow,
which then makes the movement bigger.

397
00:21:54,473 --> 00:21:59,420
Lecturer. After Tacoma, the first
response was to make decks far stiffer.

398
00:21:59,469 --> 00:22:02,140
Designers didn't change
the shape much at first.

399
00:22:02,189 --> 00:22:05,614
Instead, they hung deep trusses
underneath the road, a kind

400
00:22:05,638 --> 00:22:09,192
of open steel framework which
stopped the deck from twisting.

401
00:22:11,420 --> 00:22:15,361
Later, especially in Europe, engineers went
the other way, with slim box decks shaped

402
00:22:15,385 --> 00:22:19,427
rather like an aircraft wing, which they test
in wind tunnels before anything is built.

403
00:22:19,428 --> 00:22:23,260
Both approaches work, but the
wing-shaped decks use far less steel.

404
00:22:23,261 --> 00:22:27,100
Wind isn't the only problem, though.
People can be one, too.

405
00:22:27,349 --> 00:22:31,023
When London's Millennium Bridge
opened in 2000, which is a very

406
00:22:31,047 --> 00:22:34,792
shallow suspension bridge, it
started swaying from side to side.

407
00:22:35,129 --> 00:22:39,100
Pedestrians unconsciously fell into step
with the movement, which made it worse.

408
00:22:39,599 --> 00:22:41,744
The bridge was closed,
and there was talk of

409
00:22:41,768 --> 00:22:44,067
closing it for good,
or rebuilding it entirely.

410
00:22:44,068 --> 00:22:47,877
In the end, the fix was to install
dampers, devices that absorb

411
00:22:47,901 --> 00:22:51,912
the energy of the motion, a bit
like the shock absorbers in a car.

412
00:22:52,374 --> 00:22:53,820
Finally, maintenance.

413
00:22:54,169 --> 00:22:57,117
You might expect the biggest
long-term worry to be metal

414
00:22:57,141 --> 00:23:00,472
fatigue, the gradual weakening
from millions of loading cycles.

415
00:23:00,859 --> 00:23:04,300
In fact, for main cables,
that's a relatively minor issue.

416
00:23:04,574 --> 00:23:06,300
The real threat is corrosion.

417
00:23:07,660 --> 00:23:11,559
Water gets in between the wires,
and once they start to rust, they

418
00:23:11,583 --> 00:23:15,672
snap, one at a time, deep inside
the cable where nobody can see them.

419
00:23:15,673 --> 00:23:19,340
So how do you find a
broken wire you can't see?

420
00:23:19,340 --> 00:23:22,938
One method is to unwrap
sections of cable and inspect

421
00:23:22,962 --> 00:23:26,232
them by eye, but that's
slow and very expensive.

422
00:23:26,319 --> 00:23:29,740
X-rays have been tried, but
the cables are just too thick.

423
00:23:29,839 --> 00:23:33,100
What many bridges use
now is acoustic monitoring.

424
00:23:33,149 --> 00:23:35,782
Microphones are clamped
along the cable, and

425
00:23:35,806 --> 00:23:38,632
when a wire breaks, it
gives off a sharp crack,

426
00:23:38,719 --> 00:23:42,140
and the system records exactly
where and when it happened.

427
00:23:42,289 --> 00:23:45,060
Some bridges go further
and blow dry air through

428
00:23:45,084 --> 00:23:47,752
the cables to stop
corrosion before it starts,

429
00:23:47,839 --> 00:23:50,060
and we'll look at those
systems next week.

430
00:23:50,209 --> 00:23:53,820
For now, I'd like you to read the case
study on the reading list before Thursday.

431
00:23:54,509 --> 00:23:57,180
That is the end of the listening test.

432
00:23:57,279 --> 00:24:00,300
You now have two minutes
to check your answers.

